---
term: Conversion
section: life-cycle
order: 55
definition: The creation of a [[parent corporation]] by converting a [[contract]] among several parties into a charter, with its own organization that holds jurisdiction over all of those parties.
seeAlso:
  - parent-corporation
  - contract
  - admission
---
Conversion is the only way a parent comes to exist. William Riker called the founding agreement of a federation the *federal bargain*, and Alfred Stepan calls a federation that forms this way *coming-together*. At the [[devolution]] end of the formation gradient, the parent already exists because an earlier conversion created it.

Each party has to ratify the conversion under its own amendment procedure. A party that doesn't ratify isn't a member of the new parent. Otherwise, it would be subject to the parent's influence without being represented inside it. It stays outside as a [[neutral]], an [[associate]], or another [[status]] that the parent assigns.
